From: appkins <nbatkins@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 22 Aug 2026 13:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] DwUsbHostDxe: do not reject re-arming an async interrupt
 endpoint

A CDC-NCM gadget produced no NIC on the Pi 5's USB-C port while the same
gadget produced one on the RP1 xHCI ports, even though its HID and mass
storage functions bound correctly over DWC2. Instrumenting the bind chain
put the failure at the last step:

  NCMDIAG: MacAddress=Success DA:C0:FF:EE:10:02
  NCMDIAG: MaxBulkSize=Success size=65535
  NCMDIAG: AsyncInterrupt=Invalid Parameter interval=16
  ASSERT [NetworkCommon] DriverBinding.c(375)

The polling interval is 16 ms, well inside the accepted 1..255, and the
endpoint is a valid interrupt IN. What DwHcAsyncInterruptTransfer actually
objected to was that the endpoint already had a deferred request:

    FoundReq = DwHcFindDeferredTransfer (DwHc, DeviceAddress, EndPointAddress);
    if (IsNewTransfer) {
      if (FoundReq != NULL) {
        Status = EFI_INVALID_PARAMETER;

No other EDK2 host controller driver does this. EhcAsyncInterruptTransfer and
XhcAsyncInterruptTransfer both validate DataLength, DataToggle and
PollingInterval on the IsNewTransfer path and then create the transfer; the
only lookup either performs is on the !IsNewTransfer path, to delete. The
class drivers in MdeModulePkg are written against that contract.

NetworkCommon relies on it. DriverBindingStart arms the CDC notification
endpoint with IsNewTransfer = TRUE and the driver contains no cancelling
call at all -- not in DriverBindingStop, not anywhere -- so a second Start on
the same interface re-arms rather than re-registers. And a second Start is
not an anomaly here, it is how UsbCdcNcm is built: UsbBusDxe creates
interface handles in order, so when the CDC control interface (1) is
connected the CDC data interface (2) has no handle yet, IsUsbCdcData fails,
and Start registers a gEfiUsbIoProtocolGuid notify and returns. When the data
interface appears the notify fires, ConnectController runs Start again, this
time successfully, and NetworkCommon then binds and arms the endpoint a
second time. On EHCI and XHCI that is silently fine. Here it returned an
error, which NetworkCommon passes to ASSERT_EFI_ERROR -- fatal in DEBUG, and
in RELEASE a NIC left with a notification endpoint that was never armed.

HID and mass storage never re-register, which is why they were unaffected on
the same port and the same device.

Retire the stale request and honour the new one, matching EHCI and XHCI.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: appkins <nbatkins@gmail.com>
---
--- a/Platform/RaspberryPi/Drivers/DwUsbHostDxe/DwUsbHostDxe.c
+++ b/Platform/RaspberryPi/Drivers/DwUsbHostDxe/DwUsbHostDxe.c
@@ -1142,8 +1142,35 @@
 
   if (IsNewTransfer) {
     if (FoundReq != NULL) {
-      Status = EFI_INVALID_PARAMETER;
-      goto Done;
+      //
+      // Re-registering an endpoint that already has a deferred request is
+      // not an error. Neither EhcAsyncInterruptTransfer nor
+      // XhcAsyncInterruptTransfer has any duplicate test on the
+      // IsNewTransfer path -- they validate DataLength, DataToggle and
+      // PollingInterval and then create the transfer -- and the class
+      // drivers in MdeModulePkg are written against that behaviour.
+      //
+      // NetworkCommon is the case that matters here: DriverBindingStart
+      // arms the CDC notification endpoint with IsNewTransfer = TRUE and
+      // there is no matching cancel anywhere in the driver, not even in
+      // DriverBindingStop. UsbCdcNcm's Start runs more than once by design
+      // -- the first pass cannot pair the CDC control interface with its
+      // data interface, because UsbBusDxe connects interfaces in order and
+      // the data interface has no handle yet, so it registers a protocol
+      // notify and retries -- so the second pass arrives here and was
+      // rejected. That is why a CDC-NCM gadget produced a NIC on the RP1
+      // xHCI ports and none on the BCM2712 DWC2 port, while HID and mass
+      // storage, which never re-register, worked on both.
+      //
+      // Retire the stale request and honour the new one.
+      //
+      DEBUG ((DEBUG_INFO,
+        "%a: replacing deferred request for device %d endpoint 0x%02x\n",
+        __func__, DeviceAddress, EndPointAddress));
+      RemoveEntryList (&FoundReq->List);
+      FreePool (FoundReq->Data);
+      FreePool (FoundReq);
+      FoundReq = NULL;
     }
 
     if (DataLength == 0) {
